WEBVTT

00:00.170 --> 00:01.940
- [Lecturer] We're told
an electronic circuit

00:01.940 --> 00:06.320
has two resistors with
resistances R one and R two

00:06.320 --> 00:08.690
connected in parallel.

00:08.690 --> 00:10.620
The circuit's total resistance,

00:10.620 --> 00:15.500
R sub t or Rt is given by this formula.

00:15.500 --> 00:18.240
Suppose we increase the value R one

00:18.240 --> 00:20.483
while keeping R two constant.

00:22.474 --> 00:25.360
Does the value of R sub
t increase, decrease,

00:25.360 --> 00:27.290
or stay the same?

00:27.290 --> 00:30.720
So pause the video and see if
you can answer this question.

00:30.720 --> 00:32.410
All right, now let's work
through this together,

00:32.410 --> 00:33.980
and some of you might be familiar

00:33.980 --> 00:37.510
with the idea of an electronic
circuit and resistors,

00:37.510 --> 00:38.710
and what they represent,

00:38.710 --> 00:40.920
but you really don't
need to understand that

00:40.920 --> 00:45.010
in order to understand what's
going on in this expression.

00:45.010 --> 00:47.850
There's some quantity, R sub t,

00:47.850 --> 00:49.920
that's equal to 1 over,

00:49.920 --> 00:52.490
and then in the denominator
we have 1 over R one

00:52.490 --> 00:54.350
plus 1 over R two.

00:54.350 --> 00:56.410
So if we increase the value of R one

00:56.410 --> 00:59.850
So if we increase the value of R one

00:59.850 --> 01:03.400
while keeping R two
constant, what happens?

01:03.400 --> 01:05.140
So this is going to increase,

01:05.140 --> 01:07.790
and R two is going to be constant.

01:07.790 --> 01:08.840
So one way to think about it,

01:08.840 --> 01:10.810
we have two variables here,

01:10.810 --> 01:11.850
especially in this denominator,

01:11.850 --> 01:13.720
but really, in this entire expression,

01:13.720 --> 01:16.130
but if R two is going to be constant,

01:16.130 --> 01:19.750
we really just have to
focus our analysis on R one.

01:19.750 --> 01:22.000
If R two is constant, that
means it's just a number.

01:22.000 --> 01:24.600
It could be two, it could
be five, it could be pi.

01:24.600 --> 01:27.360
Whatever, but that is not going to change

01:27.360 --> 01:30.070
as we increase the value of R one.

01:30.070 --> 01:32.060
So let's think about
what's happening here.

01:32.060 --> 01:33.601
If R sub 1 increases,

01:33.601 --> 01:36.130
If R sub 1 increases,

01:36.130 --> 01:40.560
then what does that do to 1 over R one?

01:40.560 --> 01:42.680
Well, if you increase the denominator,

01:42.680 --> 01:46.230
then you're going to decrease
the reciprocal of that.

01:46.230 --> 01:49.240
So that means that this
whole thing right over here

01:49.240 --> 01:51.670
is going to decrease.

01:51.670 --> 01:55.040
Now, if 1 over R one is decreasing,

01:55.040 --> 01:58.810
if 1 over R one is decreasing,

01:58.810 --> 02:02.619
what's going to happen to 1
over R one plus 1 over R two?

02:02.619 --> 02:04.680
what's going to happen to 1
over R one plus 1 over R two?

02:04.680 --> 02:08.600
Will this entire expression
increase or decrease?

02:08.600 --> 02:10.760
Well, this part is staying constant.

02:10.760 --> 02:11.850
R two is constant.

02:11.850 --> 02:13.450
So 1 over R two is constant.

02:13.450 --> 02:15.180
Just imagine, R two could be two or three.

02:15.180 --> 02:18.140
So this should just be 1/2
or 1/3 or whatever it is,

02:18.140 --> 02:22.170
while over here, this part of
the expression is going down.

02:22.170 --> 02:23.790
So if you're taking the sum of two things,

02:23.790 --> 02:25.620
one part's going down,
the other part's constant,

02:25.620 --> 02:28.570
then that means this whole
thing is going to be going down.

02:28.570 --> 02:30.310
So the entire denominator

02:30.310 --> 02:33.560
of this entire thing is going down.

02:33.560 --> 02:36.950
Now, if the entire
denominator is going down,

02:36.950 --> 02:41.050
if 1 over R one plus 1 over R two,

02:41.050 --> 02:44.990
if this whole thing is going down,

02:44.990 --> 02:47.210
what's going to happen to
the reciprocal of that?

02:47.210 --> 02:51.390
1 over 1 over R one plus 1 over R two.

02:51.390 --> 02:52.970
Well, if something is going down,

02:52.970 --> 02:56.610
the reciprocal of that is going to go up.

02:56.610 --> 02:59.130
If you get smaller and
smaller denominators,

02:59.130 --> 03:02.320
1 over that is going to be
a larger and larger value.

03:02.320 --> 03:06.150
So the value or Rt increases

03:06.150 --> 03:06.983
if R one increases and R two is constant.

03:06.983 --> 03:11.320
if R one increases and R two is constant.

03:11.320 --> 03:13.330
And for those of you who
know about resistance,

03:13.330 --> 03:16.660
which is really how well

03:16.660 --> 03:18.400
a current can flow through a circuit,

03:18.400 --> 03:19.940
that will also make intuitive sense,

03:19.940 --> 03:22.930
but you don't need to
understand resistance

03:22.930 --> 03:25.953
to analyze this mathematically.
